.TH GROUPS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
groups \- print the group of a user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B groups
.RI [ user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B groups
prints the name of the group that the current user, or
.IR user ,
belongs to, followed by a newline, on standard output.
It is the
.BR id (1)
applet run under another name, and it prints what
.B id \-Gn
prints.
.PP
Windows has no Unix users or groups, so busybox emulates two users.
Each is the only member of a group with the same name and ID:
.IP \(bu 2
The current Windows user, with ID 4095.
Its name is the account name that Windows reports, without a domain, with
each space replaced by an underscore.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR root ,
with ID 0.
.PP
A process is
.BR root ,
and not the current user, when it is elevated and the Administrators
group is enabled in its access token.
With no
.IR user ,
.B groups
therefore prints
.B root
in an elevated process, and the user name otherwise.
With a
.IR user ,
whether the process is elevated does not matter.
The current user's name, compared case-sensitively, gives that name,
.B root
gives
.BR root ,
and any other name is an error.
.PP
The Windows groups that the account belongs to, such as Administrators
and Users, are never reported.
Further operands after
.I user
are ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
Apart from
.BR \-\-help ,
.B groups
takes no options.
Any other argument that begins with a dash, other than
.B \-\-
and a lone
.BR \- ,
is an unknown option and is rejected with a usage message.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and a lone
.B \-
is taken as a
.IR user ,
which is unknown.
Options are recognized after the first operand too, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first operand ends the options, so that a later argument
such as
.B \-x
is an operand and is ignored.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when
.B groups
starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, a
.I user
that is neither the current user nor
.BR root ,
an error writing standard output, or a group with no name, as described
in
.BR NOTES .
.PP
Each failure is reported on standard error, and an unknown option is
followed by a usage message:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
groups: unknown option \-\- \fIx\fR
groups: unknown user \fIuser\fR
groups: standard output: \fIreason\fR
groups: unknown ID \fIid\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
The unknown option message begins with the command as it was invoked,
in lowercase, with forward slashes, and without
.BR .exe ,
so it includes a directory when the caller gave one.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the group of the current user, here a user named
.BR jdoe :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ groups
jdoe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the group of
.BR root ,
which is known whether or not the process is elevated:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ groups root
root
.EE
.RE
.PP
Any other user is unknown:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ groups alice
groups: unknown user alice
.EE
.RE
.PP
Tell a script whether it runs as
.BR root :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ test "$(groups)" = root && echo elevated
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR groups :
.IP \(bu 2
It reports one emulated group, not the account's groups.
.IP \(bu 2
It takes one
.IR user .
GNU
.B groups
takes several, and starts each line with the user name and a colon.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the current user and
.B root
are known.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-help
is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
The name comes from Windows, not from
.BR USER ,
.BR USERNAME ,
or
.BR LOGNAME .
It is that of the account without its domain, so
.IB domain \e name
is unknown.
A name with spaces, such as
.BR "Jane Doe" ,
becomes
.BR Jane_Doe ,
and
.B "Jane Doe"
is then an unknown user.
.PP
Elevated means started with administrator rights, as from a console
opened with Run as administrator, or by
.BR su (1).
A command that
.BR drop ,
.BR cdrop ,
or
.B pdrop
starts from an elevated console is not
.BR root ,
because those commands run it with a reduced token.
.PP
When Windows does not report a user name, the group of the current user
has no name.
.B groups
with no
.I user
in a process that is not
.B root
then prints the ID,
.BR 4095 ,
reports
.B unknown ID
on standard error, and exits with status 1.
.PP
The user name is read in the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10
version 1903 and later, so on those versions it may contain any Unicode
characters.
On older versions it is limited to the system code page.
When standard output is a console, the name is converted from the ANSI
code page for display.
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR whoami (1)
